    14	# Design analysis for the combo variant
    15	# -------------------------------------
    16	# a) Concrete behavior and trace
    17	#    - The base task had one legend per rule:
    18	#        source -> target -> anchor -> nearby teal stencil
    19	#      and every scattered source pixel simply received that stencil translated so
    20	#      the legend anchor landed on the source pixel.
    21	#    - Here every scattered source is a 2-cell object:
    22	#        {source-colored center} + {fixed marker-colored neighbor}
    23	#      so the marker side supplies a visible base direction and also becomes the
    24	#      placement anchor.
    25	#    - The legend's source->target direction still identifies the rule, but now it
    26	#      also contributes an extra quarter-turn code. We keep the D16 mapping:
    27	#        source left of target  -> extra turn 0
    28	#        source above target    -> extra turn 1
    29	#        source right of target -> extra turn 2
    30	#        source below target    -> extra turn 3
    31	#    - To integrate D4, the final transform is not "just rotate the raw offsets".
    32	#      Instead the stencil is stored in a local (forward, left) frame. At stamp
    33	#      time the marker direction gives the base forward axis; the legend direction
    34	#      rotates that axis by 0/90/180/270; directions 2 and 3 also activate a
    35	#      mirror across the final forward axis. Therefore the same marker direction
    36	#      can lead to different absolute outputs depending on the legend, and the
    37	#      same legend can still produce several outputs because different markers
    38	#      change the base axis.
    39	#    - Example:
    40	#      Suppose the local stencil relative to anchor is {(0,0), (1,0), (1,1)} in
    41	#      (forward,left) coordinates. If a scattered source has its marker on the
    42	#      east side, then:
    43	#        * legend pair east  -> use forward=east, no mirror
    44	#        * legend pair south -> use forward=south, no mirror
    45	#        * legend pair west  -> use forward=west, mirror
    46	#      All three come from the same source+marker object type, but the legend adds
    47	#      an extra transform layer beyond the D3 shift.
    48	# b) Complexity calibration
    49	#    - The marker has only four possible sides and the legend direction has only
    50	#      four possible directions. That is enough to express the intended combined
    51	#      transform while staying human-trackable.
    52	#    - To keep D4 visible, the generator only emits stencil shapes whose 8 dihedral
    53	#      variants are all distinct. Symmetric stencils would collapse the new logic.
    54	#    - Each rule gets several scattered source+marker objects and uses at least two
    55	#      marker directions; grids with multiple rules also use multiple legend
    56	#      directions, including mirrored cases when possible.
    57	# c) Hidden assumptions broken in the base solver
    58	#    - Base rule discovery treated every non-bg/non-teal color as a possible legend
    59	#      member. That fails once marker pixels are introduced, so marker_color must be
    60	#      excluded from legend discovery.
    61	#    - Base stamping aligned anchor -> source cell. That is no longer true: anchor
    62	#      must align to the marker cell.
    63	#    - Base stamping also used raw absolute stencil coordinates. Here that would
    64	#      destroy the rotation/flip semantics, so rules must first convert stencil
    65	#      cells into a legend-relative local frame.
    66	#    - Base preservation copied any non-legend non-source colored cell. Marker
    67	#      pixels would leak into the output unless they are explicitly removed.
    68	#    - A naive fusion of D3 and D16 could still reduce to pure rotation. The combo
    69	#      solver therefore includes a mirror bit derived from the legend direction, and
    70	#      the generator guarantees asymmetric stencils so this extra degree of freedom
    71	#      visibly matters.
    72	# d) Placement legality
    73	#    - Legend examples are placed with full spacing so no scattered source can form
    74	#      a false source->target pair that accidentally points to a teal component.
    75	#    - Each source+marker object is placed atomically, both cells must fit, and its
    76	#      future output stencil must also fit fully in-bounds.
    77	#    - Future output stamps are kept disjoint from one another and from occupied
    78	#      input structures. This is stricter than the base task but keeps the fused
    79	#      logic legible and avoids ambiguous overlaps.
    80	# e) Visual observability
    81	#    - The input visibly contains two directional cues: the marker side on each
    82	#      scattered source and the direction of each legend color pair.
    83	#    - A human can compare repeated source+marker objects of the same color and see
    84	#      that marker direction changes orientation, then compare different legends and
    85	#      see that the legend direction adds another transform on top of that.
    86	#    - No rule depends on invisible facts such as numeric color ordering.
    87	# f) Visual cleanliness
    88	#    - Only one extra marker color is introduced, teal remains legend-only, and
    89	#      scattered objects are sparse 2-cell motifs rather than denser seeds.
    90	#    - Output stamps are separated and in-bounds, so the final recolored shapes are
